# Fixtures are bytes, not text.
#
# Almost everything under a fixtures/ directory is a byte-exact input to a
# signature or digest check: a Rekor checkpoint is a signed note, TUF metadata
# is hashed by the target that names it, a proof record is compared verbatim
# against what a zone serves. Git's end-of-line translation rewrites those
# bytes on a CRLF checkout, and every one of those checks then fails — which
# is exactly what happened on Windows CI, where a checkpoint arrived with a
# 0x0D before each 0x0A and `trusted_root.json` was 126 bytes longer than the
# metadata said it should be.
#
# `-text` disables that translation in both directions, so these files are
# checked out byte-identical on every platform.
**/tests/fixtures/** -text
**/test/fixtures/** -text

# Embedded at compile time and checked the same way: the TUF root the client
# pins is parsed into canonical JSON and its signatures verified over those
# exact bytes, and the trusted root beside it is a *hashed target* whose
# SHA-256 `targets.json` names — a CRLF checkout is a different file.
crates/synch-net/src/sigstore_tuf_root.json -text
crates/synch-net/src/sigstore_trusted_root.json -text

# The control plane's copy of that same root, shipped in priv/ because Gleam
# has no include-bytes. Its signatures are checked over canonical JSON built
# from these bytes, so a CRLF checkout is a root that verifies nothing.
control-plane/priv/tuf/sigstore_tuf_root.json -text
